Ritemark 2.0 editor groups and tab row (Sprint 132, step 1)
Each editor group is its own desk: a group with a neighbour on its left or above steps in by 8, and a desk at
the window's left or right edge stays 8 from it (editorGroupView.ts asks its part for its neighbours,
editor.ts). The tab row is the desk's 44 strip, with tabs 36 high at its foot (editorTabsControl.ts); each
tab is as wide as its whole name, 120 to 200, and a tab's tooltip shows its path inside the project (R7).
When the row is full, the tabs used longest ago fold into "N ⌄" (ritemarkFoldedTabs.ts) before the + at the
right end of the row, which makes a new Text, Table, Web page or Terminal tab (ritemarkNewTab.ts, #199).
Home and Tasks keep the front of the row (R9): once pinned, a person or a command cannot close, unpin or move
them to another group, and no tab moves in front of them; only the extension disposes them. A move command
that made a new group for them takes the empty group away again (editorCommands.ts).
A terminal is a tab (R8; Jarmo, 17 October): ritemarkTerminalTabs.ts moves any terminal that lands in the
panel onto the desk, names new ones "Terminal", "Terminal 2", and tracks whether each runs a command; the
terminal's bar (ritemarkTerminalBar.ts, in terminalEditor.ts) shows Running and Stop, and a busy terminal's
tab carries the working dot (terminalEditorInput.ts). A named terminal keeps its name as the title's source
when the shell reports its folder, so it comes back named after a reload (terminalInstance.ts).
The terminal's bar shows the shell's name as a plain label in step 1 (its menu of other shells comes later); it
keeps the last name seen, since on Windows the shell type clears while a command runs. A terminal tab's menu
adds only Rename to the usual tab items: no move into the panel, no Change Color, Change Icon or Size to Content
Width (terminalMenus.ts; Windows round 1 and the Experience lead).
Closing a terminal whose command still runs asks first, in experience 31's words ("Close Terminal 2?", Close terminal,
Don't ask again; terminalEditorInput.ts), also from the bar's Close, which closes the tab (ritemarkTerminalBar.ts).
Sprint 133: an empty desk offers the +'s first three as tiles, Text, Table and Web page, which run the +'s own
commands in that desk (ritemarkEmptyDesk.ts, in the group's watermark; drawn by 018's stylesheet; R6). The tabs ask
the resource label for the file's extension apart from its name, so 018's stylesheet mutes it (R13).
Sprint 134: the Task board tab carries the Tasks place's count, read from the Tasks container's badge (the extension
sets it), so the tab and the place never differ; its tooltip and accessible name say it in words, "Task board, 1 waits for
you" (ritemarkFoldedTabs.ts, multiEditorTabsControl.ts; drawn by 018's stylesheet; R6).
Tests: services/editor/test/browser/ritemarkPermanentTabs.test.ts (R9),
contrib/terminal/test/browser/ritemarkTerminalTabs.test.ts (R8) and test/browser/parts/editor/ritemarkTabCount.test.ts
(Sprint 134, R6); run with ./scripts/test.sh --grep Ritemark.
Sprint 134 also: the Task board tab's count (R6), and ritemark.newTab (File ▸ New tab, ⌘T / Ctrl+T) clicks the +
of the desk in front (ritemarkNewTab.ts; R21).
Built from the Experience lead's proof of concept (branch poc/2.0-surfaces), with the DOM walks replaced.

